-- Passage wg11-writing-with-evidence-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('27392148-b5b7-5528-9db6-194b6a6c773f', 'wg11-writing-with-evidence-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g11-writing-with-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Draft: What the Lichen Reveals$q$, $q$(1) In her article "Gray Crust, Clean Air," ecologist Ingrid Solberg describes a five-year survey of the lichens growing on street trees in the city of Port Avalon. (2) Lichens are interesting organisms that come in many colors and shapes. (3) Solberg explains that leafy lichens absorb water and nutrients directly from the air, so they cannot filter out pollutants the way rooted plants can. (4) Because of this, she notes, "the leafy species are the first to disappear when nitrogen levels rise and the last to return when they fall." (5) This detail suggests that the presence or absence of certain lichens can tell residents something real about the air they breathe.

(6) Solberg's survey data support this idea. (7) Her team recorded leafy lichens on only 12 percent of trees along the harbor highway but on 64 percent of trees in a park two miles inland. (8) ____, after the city replaced its diesel buses with electric ones in 2019, leafy lichens reappeared on several trees along the main bus route within three years. (9) The change on the bus route matters because it shows lichens responding to one specific improvement, not just to the many differences between a park and a highway. (10) Solberg also mentions that one oak near the harbor is more than a century old.

(11) In the end, lichens are a good thing to have in a city.$q$, 'draft')
