  ('2a8484f3-1717-59ee-b34d-002e4da75694', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g11-writing-with-evidence'), null, 'hard',
   $q$Which sentence would best conclude the draft without claiming more than the evidence shows?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Castellanos's account shows that Ferrante kept weather diaries at Lake Tamsin from 1871 to 1912.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"summary-not-analysis"},{"text":"Castellanos's account shows that Ferrante's diaries became trustworthy evidence because Bello checked them against a second source and removed weak readings.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Castellanos's account shows that Lake Tamsin will stop freezing completely within the next few decades.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Castellanos's account shows that any old diary is just as accurate as the instruments used by scientists today.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overgeneralization"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Castellanos's account shows that Ferrante's diaries became trustworthy evidence because Bello checked them against a second source and removed weak readings.$q$, $q$The first choice restates the claim and names the two checks that made the records trustworthy, staying within the evidence. The draft reports one carefully tested diary, so it cannot support a claim about every old diary.$q$, 'draft', 'e30d68c0-c979-5c17-a80e-dae56e7cea1f')
