   $q$A classmate suggests replacing sentence 4 with Castellanos's remark that Ferrante "rarely missed a morning in forty-one years." Why would this be a weaker choice?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It shows Ferrante's dedication but not that her readings were accurate, which the claim about testing records requires.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It is more specific than sentence 4, and specific evidence is less convincing than general evidence.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-principle"},{"text":"It contradicts sentence 9, which proves that Ferrante never took any readings herself.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-text"},{"text":"It is a quotation, and the writer has already used too many quotations in the first paragraph.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misidentifies-problem"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It shows Ferrante's dedication but not that her readings were accurate, which the claim about testing records requires.$q$, $q$Sentence 5 already points out that being hardworking is not the same as being reliable, and only the comparison with the government station tests accuracy. Sentence 9 says students took readings only in some years, not that Ferrante never did.$q$, 'draft', 'e30d68c0-c979-5c17-a80e-dae56e7cea1f')
